{
 "language_code": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Parashurama killed the king who once captured Ravana, but he never fought Ravana himself. Why? Some people say it was Ravana's boon. Ravana asked Brahma to protect him from gods and demons. He did not ask for protection from humans. Parashurama was a human, so the boon was not the reason. Some people say Ravana was a Brahmin, so Parashurama left him alone. But no old book says this. The real reason is simple. Parashurama had already stopped fighting. He had given the whole earth to the sage Kashyapa and moved to Mount Mahendra. Years later, Rama broke Shiva's bow. On his way home, Parashurama stopped him on the road. He gave Rama the bow of Vishnu and told him to string it. Rama did it easily. Then Rama aimed an arrow at the powers Parashurama had earned through many years of prayer. Parashurama gave them up. He said it was no shame to lose to Vishnu himself. Rama was Vishnu, born as a human. The gods had asked him to be born to kill Ravana. So killing Ravana was Rama's job, not Parashurama's. That is why Parashurama, who killed the king who captured Ravana, never fought Ravana himself. That job was Rama's.",
   "start_time": 0.12,
   "end_time": 73.08,
